  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $4.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}a+2b=5 & |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ -3a+8b=-4\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}3a+8b=20 \\\\ -3a+8b=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $3a$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-3a$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $a$-variable, and allows us to solve for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3a}+8b & =20 \\\\ -\\cancel{3a}+8b & =-4 \\\\ 16b & =16 \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $b=1$ into the equation $-3a+8b=-4$ to solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a+8b & =-4 \\\\ -3a+8\\cdot 1 & =-4 \\\\ -3a+8 & =-4 \\\\ -3a & =-12 \\\\ a & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=4$ and $b=1.$",
